            "text": "I added a v2.4.0 section to the top of `RELEASE_NOTES.md`, using the same format as the earlier releases. The version and date come from the `VERSION` and `RELEASE_DATE` files.\n\n```\nv2.4.0 - 2026-10-20\n  * BREAKING: Remove the deprecated --legacy flag from sync (ACME-421)\n  * CSV exporter for the report command (ACME-412)\n  * Retry uploads on HTTP 503 with backoff (ACME-415)\n  * Fix crash when the config file is empty (ACME-419)\n  * Fix wrong totals in the monthly summary for leap years (ACME-423)\n```\n\n**Version number:** ACME-421 is marked `!`, which means it's a breaking change: it removes the `--legacy` flag from `sync`. If you follow semantic versioning, a breaking change normally makes this 3.0.0, not 2.4.0. I left the version as 2.4.0 because that's what `VERSION` says and it's your call. If you switch to 3.0.0, update `VERSION` and the heading in the notes. Either way, you may want to tell anyone still using `--legacy` before the release.\n\nI put the breaking change first so it's easy to spot, then the features, then the fixes. Contributor names aren't included because the earlier releases don't list them. I can add them if you want."
